.TH AGENT-HOOK-PERMISSION-REQUEST-CLAUDE 1 "2026-11-18" "agentguard" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agent-hook-permission-request-claude \- allow Claude Code bypass-mode Bash and Monitor prompts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agent-hook-permission-request-claude
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agent-hook-permission-request-claude
is sourced by
.BR agent-hook-permission-request (1)
when the active runtime is Claude and
.B AGENTGUARD_PERMISSION_REQUEST_ALLOW
is enabled. It is not a standalone hook adapter and should not be configured
directly as the PermissionRequest command.
.PP
It calls
.B _hook_allow_permission
when the payload is one JSON object whose
.B permission_mode
is exactly
.B bypassPermissions
and whose
.B tool_name
is exactly
.B Bash
or
.BR Monitor .
Anything else, including a payload that cannot be parsed, leaves the prompt to
the user.
.PP
In that mode Claude Code still asks before some commands, such as an
.B rm
whose target Claude cannot rule out, and a headless
.B claude \-p
run, a nested session, or a teammate that nobody answers denies them. With the
knob on, they run. The extension does not look at the command or at why Claude
asked, so it answers any Bash or Monitor prompt raised in bypass mode,
including one from an explicit
.B ask
permission rule.
.B agent-hook-pre-bash
still blocks the destructive
.B rm -rf
targets listed under Default Policy (see
.BR agent-hook-pre-bash (1)).
.PP
Claude's permission-modes documentation states that its own
.B CLAUDE_CODE_EVEN_MORE_DANGEROUSLY_SKIP_PERMISSIONS
has no effect while a PermissionRequest hook with any matcher is registered
(AgentGuard's telemetry recorder is one), in a session started from another
session, or when set in a settings file's
.B env
block.
.PP
The Claude Code integration registers the hook with matcher
.BR Bash|Monitor ;
Monitor must be named for an allow to apply to Monitor commands.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Always; the extension only decides whether to call
.BR _hook_allow_permission .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agent-hook-permission-request (1),
.BR agent-hook-pre-bash (1)
